## 55. UNION TERRITORIES (ARTICLES 239 TO 241)

**[SYL: GS2-G.1] | [LAXMIKANTH: Chapter 40] | [UNION TERRITORIES, CENTRALLY ADMINISTERED, NCT DELHI, LEGISLATIVE ASSEMBLIES]**

### 55.1. Constitutional Basis
- **Articles**: Articles 239 to 241 in **Part VIII** of the Constitution deal with Union Territories.
- **Article 1**: Territory of India comprises (a) territories of states, (b) union territories, (c) territories that may be acquired by Government of India.
- **Nature**: Union Territories are under direct control and administration of Central Government. Hence, also called **'centrally administered territories'**. This constitutes a conspicuous departure from federalism in India - relationship between Centre and UTs is **unitary** in nature.

### 55.2. Creation of Union Territories
- **Historical Background**: During British rule, certain areas were 'scheduled districts' (1874), later 'chief commissioners' provinces. After independence, placed as Part 'C' States and Part 'D' Territories. In 1956, constituted as Union Territories by **7th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1956** and **States Reorganisation Act, 1956**.
- **Evolution**: Some UTs elevated to statehood (Himachal Pradesh, Manipur, Tripura, Mizoram, Arunachal Pradesh, Goa). Territories acquired from Portuguese (Goa, Daman & Diu, Dadra & Nagar Haveli) and French (Puducherry) constituted as UTs.
- **Present UTs** (9): (1) Andaman & Nicobar Islands (1956), (2) Delhi (1956), (3) Lakshadweep (1956), (4) Dadra & Nagar Haveli (1961), (5) Daman & Diu (1962), (6) Puducherry (1962), (7) Chandigarh (1966), (8) Jammu & Kashmir (2019), (9) Ladakh (2019). 
- **Name changes**: Lakshadweep was earlier Laccadive, Minicoy & Amindivi Islands (renamed 1973). Delhi redesignated as **National Capital Territory of Delhi (NCT)** in 1992. Puducherry earlier Pondicherry (renamed 2006).
- **Reasons for creation**:
  1. **Political and administrative consideration** - Delhi, Chandigarh
  2. **Cultural distinctiveness** - Puducherry, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu
  3. **Strategic importance** - Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Lakshadweep
  4. **Special treatment/care of backward and tribal people** - Mizoram, Manipur, Tripura, Arunachal Pradesh (later became states)

#### 55.2.1. J&K Reorganisation, 2019
- **Jammu and Kashmir Reorganisation Act, 2019** bifurcated erstwhile J&K state into two UTs w.e.f. 31st October 2019:
  1. **Union Territory of Jammu and Kashmir** - with a **legislature**
  2. **Union Territory of Ladakh** - **without a legislature**
- **Rationale**: Ladakh's vast area, sparse population, difficult terrain and long-pending demand for UT status; prevailing internal security situation due to cross-border terrorism necessitated separate UT for J&K.

### 55.3. Administration of Union Territories
- **Articles 239-241**: Deal with administration of UTs. No uniformity in administrative system.
- **President's role**: Every UT administered by **President acting through an administrator** appointed by him. Administrator is an **agent of President**, not head of state like Governor.
- **Designation of administrator**: President specifies designation - may be **Lieutenant Governor**, **Chief Commissioner** or **Administrator**.
- **Current designations**:
  - **Lieutenant Governor**: Delhi, Puducherry, Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Jammu & Kashmir, Ladakh
  - **Administrator**: Chandigarh, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu, Lakshadweep
- **Governor as administrator**: President can appoint Governor of a state as administrator of adjoining UT. In that capacity, Governor acts **independently** of his Council of Ministers.
- **Concurrent charge**: Governor of Punjab is concurrently Administrator of Chandigarh. Administrator of Dadra & Nagar Haveli is concurrently Administrator of Daman & Diu.

#### 55.3.1. UTs with Legislature
Three UTs have legislative assembly and Council of Ministers headed by Chief Minister:
1. **Puducherry** - since 1963 (Assembly strength 30)
2. **National Capital Territory of Delhi** - since 1992 (Assembly strength 70)
3. **Jammu and Kashmir** - since 2019 (Assembly strength 83)

Remaining six UTs have **no** popular political institutions. Creation of local legislatures does **not** diminish supreme control of President and Parliament.

#### 55.3.2. Parliamentary & Presidential Powers
- **Parliamentary laws**: Parliament can make laws on **any subject** of the three lists (including State List) for all UTs. This power extends even to those with local legislatures.
- **Local legislatures' powers**:
  - **Puducherry**: Can make laws on subjects in **State List** and **Concurrent List**
  - **Delhi (NCT)**: Can make laws on subjects in **State List** and **Concurrent List** **except** Public Order, Police and Land
  - **Jammu & Kashmir**: Can make laws on subjects in **State List** (except Public Order and Police) and **Concurrent List**
- **Presidential regulations**: President can make regulations for peace, progress and good government of **Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Lakshadweep, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu and Ladakh**. For **Puducherry**, President can legislate by regulations only when Assembly is **suspended or dissolved**. A regulation has same force as an Act of Parliament and can repeal/amend any Act of Parliament in relation to these UTs.
- **High Courts**: Parliament can establish High Court for a UT or place it under jurisdiction of High Court of adjacent state. **Delhi** has its own High Court (since 1966). Others placed under jurisdiction of adjacent High Courts. **J&K High Court** is common High Court for J&K and Ladakh UTs.
- **Acquired territories**: Constitutional provisions for UTs also apply to acquired territories.

### 55.4. Special Provisions for National Capital Territory of Delhi (69th Amendment Act, 1991)
- **Status**: Union Territory of Delhi redesignated as **National Capital Territory of Delhi**. Administrator designated as **Lieutenant Governor**.
- **Legislative Assembly**: Strength fixed at **70 members**, directly elected. Can make laws on all State List and Concurrent List subjects **except** Public Order, Police and Land. Laws of Parliament prevail over those made by Assembly.
- **Council of Ministers**: Strength not exceeding **10%** of total strength of Assembly (max 7) - Chief Minister + other ministers. Chief Minister appointed by **President** (not by Lt. Governor). Other ministers appointed by President on advice of CM. Ministers hold office during pleasure of President. Council collectively responsible to Assembly.
- **Relationship with Lt. Governor**: Council of Ministers headed by CM aids and advises Lt. Governor in exercise of his functions **except** where he is required to act in his **discretion**. In case of difference of opinion between Lt. Governor and Ministers, Lt. Governor shall refer matter to **President** for decision and act accordingly.
- **President's Rule**: If administration cannot be carried on as per provisions, President can suspend operation of these provisions and make incidental/consequential provisions. Can be done on report of Lt. Governor or otherwise. Resembles Article 356.
- **Ordinance-making power**: Lt. Governor empowered to promulgate ordinances during recess of Assembly. Ordinance has same force as Act of Assembly. Must be approved by Assembly within **6 weeks** from reassembly. Can be withdrawn anytime. Cannot promulgate/withdraw without **prior permission of President** when Assembly dissolved/suspended.

### 55.5. Advisory Committees for Union Territories
- **Nodal Ministry**: Ministry of Home Affairs is nodal ministry for all UT matters relating to legislation, finance/budget, services, appointment of Lt. Governors/Administrators.
- **HMAC/AAC**: For UTs **without legislature** (Andaman & Nicobar, Chandigarh, Dadra & Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu, Lakshadweep, Ladakh), forum of **Home Minister's Advisory Committee (HMAC)** / **Administrator's Advisory Committee (AAC)** exists.
- **Composition**: HMAC chaired by Union Home Minister; AAC chaired by Administrator of concerned UT. MPs and elected members from local bodies (District Panchayats, Municipal Councils) are members.
- **Function**: Discuss general issues relating to social and economic development of UTs.

### 55.6. Comparative Analysis: States vs Union Territories
| Feature | States | Union Territories |
|---|---|---|
| **Relationship with Centre** | Federal | Unitary |
| **Distribution of power** | Share power with Centre (division of powers) | Under direct control and administration of Centre |
| **Autonomy** | Have autonomy | No autonomy |
| **Administrative set-up** | Uniform in nature | No uniformity in administrative set-up |
| **Executive head** | Governor (Constitutional head) | Administrator/Lt. Governor/Chief Commissioner (Agent of President) |
| **Parliament's legislative power** | Cannot make laws on State List subjects except under extraordinary circumstances (Article 249, 250, 252, 253) | Can make laws on **any subject** of three lists (including State List) for UTs |

### 55.7. Articles Related to Union Territories at a Glance
| Article | Subject-matter |
|---|---|
| **239** | Administration of Union Territories |
| **239A** | Creation of local Legislatures or Council of Ministers or both for certain Union Territories (added by 7th Amendment Act, 1956) |
| **239AA** | Special provisions with respect to Delhi (added by 69th Amendment Act, 1991) |
| **239AB** | Provision in case of failure of constitutional machinery |
| **239B** | Power of administrator to promulgate Ordinances during recess of Legislature |
| **240** | Power of President to make regulations for certain Union Territories |
| **241** | High Courts for Union Territories |

